message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
title: "Deterministic Zero-Trust: Trust as a Computable, Decaying, Revocable, Cryptographically-Bounded Physical Quantity"
authors:
  - family-names: "Taylor"
    given-names: "David W."
    affiliation: "Independent Researcher"
type: article
date-released: "2026-08-12"
license: CC-BY-4.0
keywords:
  - zero-trust architecture
  - deterministic security
  - tau conservation
  - KMS token scoping
  - Merkle provenance
  - antimatter annihilation
  - cascading invalidation
  - execution firewalls
  - post-quantum cryptography
  - formal verification
abstract: >-
  We introduce Deterministic Zero-Trust (DZT), a security architecture where
  trust is not a confidence score but a physical quantity governed by
  conservation laws, mandatory decay, cryptographic scoping, and cascading
  invalidation. We prove that DZT provides seven formal security guarantees
  unachievable by probabilistic systems. The architecture is demonstrated
  across 15 implemented security primitives, each proven by running code.
